// g_tearingDown is untouched and keeps every reader it had.
// P6q (review-p6j CRASH-1) - AND THE "IT CANNOT LATCH" ARGUMENT THAT USED TO BE WRITTEN HERE IS WITHDRAWN.
// It said: TeardownBroadcastLateFlags has no thread guard and no early return, and both detours call it unconditionally
// after orig_*, so the lower runs wherever the raise ran. Both halves are true and neither is the question. "No
// early return" is a statement about the FUNCTION; being reached is a statement about the CALL, and there is a
// third way a call is skipped - orig_resetGame / orig_worldTeardown not returning normally. An SEH fault or a C++
// throw propagating out of the engine's own teardown unwinds straight past the flush and past the lower, and this
// flag then stands for the life of the process: StoreTearingDown() answers 1 forever, MayInventFromView returns
// kInventTeardown for every call, and every town the player walks into has its bar-fly list drained irreversibly -
// with no crash, no error line and nothing in the log but a climbing teardown[refused,barFlies].
// g_tearingDown never had that exposure, for a reason that has nothing to do with its own teardown path: it has a
// SECOND, unconditional clear at the first statement of detour_loadFromDisk, so however a teardown ends the flag is
// down again by the next world's first squad container. P6j narrowed the span by giving the new flag ONE lower and
// removed exactly that property (the F125 -> F177 shape: fixing the instance is not fixing the possibility). Both
// are restored here - the lower runs in a __finally in both detours, and the loadFromDisk clear is back, so any
// latch is bounded by "until the next world loads", strictly inside the span this flag is not supposed to cover.
// AND IT IS AN INTERLOCKED long, NOT A PLAIN bool (review-p6j LOW-1). The readers that matter are worker threads.
// P6x (review-p6q MEDIUM-2) - AND IT IS A DEPTH, NOT A FLAG, WHICH IS THE HALF OF CRASH-1 THE __finally DID NOT
// CLOSE. Both teardown functions are hooked. If the engine ever reaches _clearAndDestroyGameWorldStuff from inside
// orig_resetGame, the INNER detour lowers while the OUTER orig_* is still freeing the world - the gate down for
// exactly the window it exists for - and the __finally makes that inner lower MORE certain to run, not less.
// TeardownBroadcast increments; TeardownBroadcastLateFlags decrements; StoreTearingDown() is "depth > 0". The gate
// therefore stands until the OUTERMOST teardown has finished. NOT OBSERVED: all 62 [STORE] world teardown lines in
// evidence/ say resetGame and _clearAndDestroyGameWorldStuff appears in no log, so this is a structural repair and
// is recorded as one, not as a fix for a measured failure.
