// E29 (review-p5r MEDIUM-1): THE SECOND DRAIN - THE ONE THAT CATCHES WHAT THE ENGINE'S TEARDOWN ITSELF QUEUED.
// TeardownBroadcast runs BEFORE orig_*: it flushes the backlog the world built up to the moment the teardown began (a
// save-and-quit writes its zones first, on workers, and that is the case its flush exists for), and it then clears the
// maps. Everything the engine's own teardown pushes - a streaming worker unloading a zone through detour_gdcSave, a
// destroy on a worker - lands in the outbox AFTER all of that, with nowhere to go until the next world's first StoreTick,
// by which time g_tearingDown is true and the maps have been cleared. Those entries were effectively lost, and the loss
// showed only as outboxQueued - outboxDrained with nothing naming it.
// WHAT THIS DOES NOT FIX, stated plainly because the flush above claims otherwise (F533, 2026-09-04): NO STORE TEARDOWN
// RUNS AT QUIT. The only "[STORE] world teardown" line in any run so far is the load-time resetGame; the engine's quit
// path faults inside its own save prologue at WM_CLOSE and never reaches _clearAndDestroyGameWorldStuff through this hook,
// and coop-plugin has no unload hook of its own (startPlugin is its only export). So neither the flush above nor this one
// is a "plugin exit" flush, and anything in the outbox at quit is still lost. This drain covers the world-load teardown,
// which is the one that measurably happens.
// So the outbox is drained once more here, after the engine's function has returned. What this can and cannot do:
//   * a kOutZone queued during the teardown IS still writable - the record file is on disk and ZoneSaveApply can write it.
//     This is the only place in the process where that is true.
//   * a kOutGone is drained with g_tearingDown already true and the maps already cleared, so it books goneNoIdDrained and
//     deletes nothing. That is CORRECT and not a shortfall: a group the engine destroys as part of tearing the world down
//     is not "gone" - it is being unloaded - and the main path has always answered it the same way.
// Both teardown hooks get it, because both call the same TeardownBroadcast and both are followed by the same engine work.
void FlushOutboxAfterEngineTeardown(const char* why)
{
    /* E33 (verify-p5v LOW-3): a REFUSED flush is counted and says so. A bare return here, with the counter and the
       log line both below the old zero-sample gate, meant an off-thread teardown produced no number and no line -
       and the sibling site (NoteQuitting) produced "flushed 0", which reads as "the queue was empty". */
    if (!OnMainThread()) { ++g_outboxFlushRefusedOffThread; DebugLog("[STORE] world teardown (" + std::string(why) + "): outbox NOT flushed - this is not the main thread (the drain sends and writes files, and off the main thread the senders would only re-queue what they were handed)"); return; }
    /* P6c (p5z MEDIUM-5): the scope in which a zone write is attributable to THIS flush. Main thread only (the
       refusal above returns), so a plain bool is enough. */
    g_postTeardownFlush = true;
    const long long took = StoreOutboxDrain();   /* E33 (verify-p5v MEDIUM-2): the drain's own count, not a sample taken before it */
    g_postTeardownFlush = false;
    if (took == 0) return;
    g_outboxFlushedPostTeardown += took;
    DebugLog("[STORE] world teardown (" + std::string(why) + "): flushed " + N(took) + " outbox entries the ENGINE'S OWN teardown queued after ours had already run");
}
// E33 (verify-p5v MEDIUM-1) - THE LAST TWO CLEARS, AFTER THE FLUSH THAT HAS TO READ THEM.
